Rogues are fun. Throwing +7 daggers/poisoned darts and watching
enemies die like flies is so gratifying. But wielding a powerful melee
weapon early on, with poor rogue HP and AC, is often a deathtrap. This
is in no way an effort to "fix" what I already consider a thoroughly
enjoyable class, but after reading the complete Chronicles of Amber, I
thought Merlin's weapon of choice would make an interesting rogue
weapon. Its method of us, basically, makes it a combination of a
decent thrown weapon, a pet, and an escape item, without sufficient
power to supplant any of the former.

For the backstory, Frakir is an enchanted, intelligent strangling cord
that alights on one's wrist when unused. At the owner's bidding, she
leaps onto an enemies' throat and stangles him/her, giving the owner
time to either escape or concentrate his fire on more pressing enemies
while the job is done. Unfortunately, while I'd imagine the poor foe
would be stunned/confused while gripping at his throat, he'd still
attack the player, so strangling a leocrotta or other fast enemy is
stupid, ditto for unbreathing monsters, without an escape mechanism,
and Frakir has to be retrieved before another use.

Here's the potential details: when attacking a breathing, necked
enemy, Frakir does substantial damage while affixed on the enemy's
neck, as well as stunning the enemy. She falls to the ground rather
than fastening to an unbreathing monster, since she's supposedly
intelligent. A +0 Frakir shouldn't be enough to kill a priest or
powerful caster due to healing ability, but a +7 Frakir should be
substantial enough to kill pretty much anything *eventually*. Frakir
also vibrates madly when the player approaches traps, just like a
leashed pet.

As for balance issues, yes, it would be a guaranteed kill for
shopkeepers, but so are expert daggers at +3 or more, rubber chickens,
or any pet more powerful than a large dog. If the player enchants it
in place of his dagger stack, then by golly, it *should* kill the
priest, because +3 daggers at 18 strength would almost certainly do
the same. Since it's useless against Vlad, liches, demons, or Rodney
(he's unbreathing, I recall), it remains a weak late game weapon,
merely a useful way of killing a leocrotta/minotaur/mind flayer in the
Gnomish Mines/other *single* powerful monster standing between you and
some nice goodies. I'd almost consider it a liability compared to
better weapons such as Frosty and Grayswandir, but hey, wouldn't
tossing a strangling cord at some obnoxious shopkeeper and running
like hell be fun?



